 "narration": [
  "Rhodopsin absorbs a photon, and its retinal switches from the 11-cis form to the all-trans form, which cannot be used again until it is converted back.",
  "The bond between all-trans retinal and the opsin is broken. The retinal is released, converted to all-trans retinol, and leaves the rod.",
  "A transport protein called interphotoreceptor retinoid-binding protein, or IRBP, carries it across to the retinal pigment epithelium, the RPE.",
  "In the RPE, a series of enzymes converts it back: first to 11-cis retinol, and then to 11-cis retinal.",
  "The regenerated 11-cis retinal is bound by IRBP again, and shuttled back out of the RPE.",
  "Back in the rod's outer segment, the 11-cis retinal is released and binds to an opsin, ready to absorb light again."
 ],
 "narrationAudio": [
  "data:audio/mpeg;base64,",
  "data:audio/mpeg;base64,",
  "data:audio/mpeg;base64,",
  "data:audio/mpeg;base64,",
  "data:audio/mpeg;base64,",
  "data:audio/mpeg;base64,"
 ],
 "infoOpen": false,
 "infoText": "When rhodopsin absorbs a photon, its light-catching 11-cis retinal chromophore is isomerized into all-trans retinal. This latter form cannot be used for photon absorption, and the retina undertakes a complex series of steps to convert all-trans retinal back to photo-activatable 11-cis retinal. Following binding of arrestin, the bond between all-trans retinal and the opsin is broken. The all-trans aldehyde is released and quickly converted to all-trans retinol by a membrane-bound retinol dehydrogenase. From here, a transport protein called interphotoreceptor retinoid-binding protein (IRBP) takes all-trans retinol on an inter-cellular journey into the retinal pigment epithelium, which interdigitates with photoreceptor outer segments. The retinal pigment epithelium contains three enzymes that work in sequence&nbsp;&mdash;&nbsp;lecithin retinol acyltransferase (LRAT), which converts all-trans retinol to a retinyl ester; the retinyl-ester isomerase RPE65, which converts that ester to 11-cis retinol; and 11-cis retinol dehydrogenase, which uses NAD<sup class=\"ch\">+<\/sup> to oxidize 11-cis retinol back into 11-cis retinal. The regenerated 11-cis retinal is highly insoluble and is quickly bound by IRBP, which encapsulates the molecule and makes the return trip out of the retinal pigment epithelium and back into the photoreceptor outer segment, where it is released and binds anew with an opsin.",
 "steps": [
  "Rhodopsin absorbs a photon of light and retinal changes from 11-cis to all-trans",
  "All-trans retinal is converted to all-trans retinol, which exits the rod",
  "All-trans retinol binds to IRBP, which transports it into the RPE",
  "In the RPE, all-trans retinol is converted in several steps back to 11-cis retinal",
  "11-cis retinal binds to IRBP and is shuttled out of the RPE",
  "11-cis retinal re-enters the rod where it can get activated by light again"
 ],
